  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nA cutoff upper low will remain stationary over Georgia through\nWednesday before dissipating and becoming absorbed by the broad\nupper trough moving into the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley on\nThursday. Expect mostly dry weather on the periphery of this\nfeature with diurnal cumulus for the next couple of days. There\nis a very low chance (<10%) of a shower along the\nMississippi/Alabama border and the Tennessee River for the next\ncouple of afternoons.\n\nTemperatures will slowly warm into the lower to mid 90s by\nThursday and humidity levels will start to creep back up as\nsoutherly surface winds develop. Dewpoints will be back in the\nlower to mid 70s by Thursday afternoon. Heat index values will\nstart to climb into the triple digits again, though they are\nlikely to remain below Heat Advisory criteria at most places\nthrough Saturday. However, we will need to monitor the Delta\nregion southwest of Memphis.\n\nThe broad upper trough moving into the Great Lakes and Ohio\nValley will extend into the Mid-South late Friday into Saturday.\nThis will result in isolated to scattered showers and\nthunderstorms (20-35%) across the Mid-South during that\ntimeframe. QPF amounts for the next 7 days will average less than\none-quarter inch for the most part.\n\nThe upper trough pushes east by Sunday with a broad upper ridge\nnosing in from the Southern Plains. This will result in slightly\nwarmer temperatures and mostly dry weather except for isolated\nafternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms over mainly\nNortheast Mississippi. Persistent southerly flow will result in\ndewpoints solidly in the mid 70s. As a result, the chance for\nheat index values of 105F or greater are on the increase from\nSunday to Tuesday and Heat Advisories may be needed.",
